{'id': 1899, 'role': 'tool', 'content': '{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "description": "Use when solving ARC-style continual grid puzzles.", "tags": [], "related_skills": [], "content": "---\\nname: continual-arc-solving\\ndescription: Use when solving ARC-style continual grid puzzles.\\n---\\n\\n# Continual ARC Grid Solving\\n\\nUse this class-level skill for ARC-style tasks that provide a grid, optional demonstrations, limited demo requests, and a strict machine-readable action format.\\n\\n## 1. Respect the instance protocol\\n\\n- On every turn, emit exactly one permitted action object on the final line. Do not add explanatory text when the task requests JSON only.\\n- Track wrong submissions, demo requests, and attempt cap from the latest instance state.\\n- A correct submission ends the instance; avoid unnecessary demonstrations once the rule is sufficiently identified.\\n\\n## 2. Parse grids with coordinates\\n\\n- Use zero-based `(row, column)` coordinates internally and record dimensions.\\n- Identify background, marker colors, and connected components. For each object, record color, bounding box, dimensions, and holes or irregularities.\\n- Preserve original cells by default. Build a transformed copy rather than reconstructing the whole grid by hand.\\n- Before submission, verify row count, every row width, allowed values, and that untouched regions match the input.\\n\\n## 3. Infer rules from demonstrations\\n\\n- Describe each demo as observable facts: what is added, removed, recolored, moved, or preserved. Avoid generalizing from one example prematurely.\\n- Maintain competing hypotheses when a detail is ambiguous. Identify a specific object or cell outcome where they disagree.\\n- Request another demo when it is likely to discriminate between plausible rules and the information is worth its cost. Choose a discriminator, not merely another visually similar example.\\n- Distinguish canvas/line effects from object effects. Check whether objects change when a line overlaps them, whether edge contact differs from an interior crossing, and whether line cells follow a separate rule. Treat these as hypotheses until supported by examples.\\n\\n## 4. Use feedback as evidence\\n\\n- After an incorrect submission, do not repeat the same transformation with only cosmetic changes. Compare input and candidate cell-by-cell, list exact edits, and revise a concrete assumption.\\n- If a candidate consistent with demos is rejected, audit coordinates, object extents, line endpoints, dimensions, and serialization first. If these are correct, assume the hypothesis is incomplete: request a discriminating demo or test a meaningfully different rule.\\n- Binary correct/incorrect feedback is weak but valuable evidence. Record which candidate family it rules out; do not claim certainty from it.\\n\\n## 5. Construct and validate outputs\\n\\n- Prefer a small deterministic script or structured row/column edits for grids where manual transcription is error-prone.\\n- Preserve the input and apply transformations in a clear order, checking whether overlaps make operation order significant.\\n- Inspect the final grid and validate it against all demonstrations and test dimensions before submitting.\\n- Encode boundary distinctions explicitly when the examples support them; do not assume a boundary touch acts like an interior crossing.\\n\\n## Pitfalls\\n\\n- A visually plausible rule is not necessarily verified; demonstrations can leave edge cases unresolved.\\n- Repeatedly submitting the same rejected hypothesis wastes the attempt budget. Diagnose, seek information, or change the hypothesis materially.\\n- Hand-copying many rows can introduce silent alignment errors; validate dimensions and cell positions.\\n- Never add prose, markdown fences, or a second action when the evaluator requires exactly one JSON action.\\n", "path": "puzzles/continual-arc-solving/SKILL.md", "skill_dir": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/